<!--
  ⛔ A STIPULATION, NOT A FILING. Nothing here was measured. The figures exist to put one state
  of the model in front of a checker, and that state is AN UNMEASURED CONVERSION.

  ⭐⭐⭐ WHY THE CELL WAS DARK, AND IT WAS DARK FOUR LAYERS DEEP. `asrt:Part/factor` is
  `minOccurs="0"` over `pm:StatedClaim`, so it admits THREE states and not two:

    no `factor` element        the part is ALREADY in the composed unit. phi = 1, exactly.
    `factor/claim`             a stated conversion. phi is the filed rate.
    `factor/absent`            somebody looked and could not size it.            <- this file

  The schema says so itself: "a conversion nobody has measured is `absent reason=unmeasured`
  INSIDE the `pm:StatedClaim`, a different document from a part carrying no `factor` at all."
  Three implementations below it disagreed. `pm.part` had no `factor_absent` column, so the
  first and third state were the same three NULLs; `ingest.sql` had no path for the third; and
  `tests/state_coverage.rs` collected the factor and then matched only its `Claim` arm, so the
  absence fell through uncounted. Nothing would have told you it arrived.

  ⛔⛔ AND READING IT AS phi = 1 IS THE FAILURE. `composition/converted.sqlc` coalesces a NULL
  factor to 1, which is correct for the FIRST state and asserts a rate nobody filed for this one.
  The fused figure would look perfectly ordinary.

    team-a nameplate   10 shifts per week      already in the composed unit, no factor element
    team-b nameplate   10 turnos por semana    a conversion nobody measured
    fused                              ?       not 20. Not computable.

  ⚠️ WHAT THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT DECIDE. Whether an unmeasured factor SUSPENDS the sum the way an
  unmeasured elimination does is a question the schema has not answered, and a fixture is not the
  place to answer it. The eliminations below are filed `unmeasured` so the arithmetic is switched
  off by a ground the model already recognises, leaving the factor state to be the only new thing
  here. ⭐ A fixture proves reachability, never correctness.
-->
